course: dictionary
lesson: 2410
title: Cutlery: the words for eating tools
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Cutlery is a group word for the knives, forks and spoons we use to eat and serve food. You can use it when you talk about the whole set, just as you would say furniture for chairs and tables together.
explain: «When a table is laid, every person gets a knife, a fork and a spoon: 3 pieces of cutlery for each person.» So one person's place at the table has 3 pieces of cutlery, and the word cutlery covers all of them together.
explain: «Pizza, sandwiches, chips and chicken drumsticks are finger foods, and most other foods are eaten with cutlery.» Finger foods are picked up with your hands. If a food needs a spoon or a fork, the sentence tells you it is eaten with cutlery.
fact: Cutlery means the knives, forks and spoons used for eating and serving food.
fact: Cutlery is an uncountable noun, so we say «the cutlery», not «the cutleries».
fact: Count the items with pieces: «3 pieces of cutlery» means three knives, forks or spoons.
fact: Phrases you may hear: set the cutlery, plastic cutlery, cutlery drawer, a piece of cutlery.
fact: Table manners in the UK: the fork goes in the left hand and the knife in the right; in the USA, people often cut their food and then switch the fork to the right hand.
exercise: «When a table is laid, every person gets a knife, a fork and a spoon.» What is cutlery? | The food on the plate | The chairs we sit on | The plates and cups on the table | *The knives, forks and spoons we use for eating
exercise: Which of these is a finger food, not eaten with cutlery? | *Chips | Soup | Stew | Rice pudding
exercise: In the UK, which hand usually holds the fork at the table? | Neither hand, you just use your mouth | *Left hand | Right hand | Either hand, it never matters
test: «3 pieces of cutlery» for each person at a laid table: how many pieces is that? | 2 | 5 | 4 | *3
test: Which of these is NOT a piece of cutlery? | *A plate | A fork | A spoon | A knife
exam: Cutlery is an uncountable noun. Which sentence uses it correctly? | Please set the cutlery's on the table. | Please set three cutlerys on the table. | Please set the cutleries on the table. | *Please set the cutlery on the table.
